{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_103", "type": "reasoning", "text": "OK,Low-precision approximation methods perform poorly and can't meet the NRMSE requirement. The geomloss_match_reconstruct method is the current best option with excellent performance and low overhead. I'm exploring optimizations like cutting label cost computation and adopting a hybrid approach to further improve speed.\n\nSimple batchcost approximations fail to preserve relative ordering. I've identified feasible optimization directions including proxy cost estimation and downsampled cost calculation, and will keep pursuing further performance improvements.\n\nI've clarified the difference in regularization settings between GeomLoss and canonical POT, which leads to distinct calculation results.\n\nI've confirmed that direct use of GeomLoss loss values is infeasible, as minimum cost rankings do not align with mean cost rankings. I've proposed a feasible solution of running two GeomLoss solves per pair with matched effective regularization, and found the current optimization gains are far lower than expected, likely due to the custom cost function slowing down the matched-reg GeomLoss solve. I will next verify the speed of the matched-reg solve and total time of the two GeomLoss runs.\n\nI will run detailed timing to identify bottlenecks and optimize speed.\n"}}
